===On- and offglides===
Broad ([[Velar consonant|velar]] or [[Velarization|velarized]]) consonants have a noticeable [[velar approximant|velar offglide]] ({{IPA|[ɰ]}}; a very short vowel-like sound) before [[front vowel]]s, which sounds like the English {{IPA|/w/}} but without [[Roundedness|rounding]]. Thus {{lang|ga|naoi}} {{IPA|/n̪ˠiː/}} ('nine') and {{lang|ga|caoi}} {{IPA|/kiː/}} ('way, manner') are pronounced {{IPA|[n̪ˠɰiː]}} and {{IPA|[kɰiː]}}, respectively.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Sjoestedt|1931|p=19}}</ref><ref>{{Harvnb|Sutton|1993}}</ref> This velar offglide is [[labialized]] (pronounced [w]) after [[labial consonant]]s, so {{lang|ga|buí}} {{IPA|/bˠiː/}} ('yellow') is pronounced {{IPA|[bˠwiː]}}.<ref>{{Harvnb|Sutton|1993}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Quiggin|1906|p=76}}</ref>

Similarly, slender ([[Palatal consonant|palatal]] or [[Palatalization (phonetics)|palatalized]]) consonants have a [[palatal approximant|palatal offglide]] ({{IPA|[j]}}; like English {{Anglebracket|y}}) before [[back vowel]]s, e.g. {{lang|ga|tiubh}} {{IPA|/tʲuː/}} ('thick') is pronounced {{IPA|[tʲjuː]}}.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Sé|2000|p=11}}</ref>

When a broad consonant follows a front vowel, there is a very short vowel sound {{IPA|[ə̯]}} (called an onglide) just before the consonant, e.g. {{lang|ga|díol}} {{IPA|/dʲiːl̪ˠ/}} ('sell') is pronounced {{IPA|[dʲiːə̯l̪ˠ]}}. Similarly, when a slender consonant follows a back vowel, there is an onglide {{IPA|[i̯]}} before the consonant, e.g. {{lang|ga|áit}} {{IPA|/aːtʲ/}} ('place') is pronounced {{IPA|[aːi̯tʲ]}},<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Sé|2000|p=11}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|de Bhaldraithe|1966|p=43}}</ref>{{lang|ga|óil}} {{IPA|/oːlʲ/}} ('drinking' [[genitive case|gen.]]) is pronounced {{IPA|[oːi̯lʲ]}},<ref>{{Harvcolnb|de Búrca|1958|p=59}}</ref> {{lang|ga|meabhair}} {{IPA|/mʲəuɾʲ/}} ('understanding') is {{IPA|[mʲəui̯ɾʲ]}},<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Mhac an Fhailigh|1968|p=46}}</ref> and {{lang|ga|dúinn}} {{IPA|/d̪ˠuːn̠ʲ/}} ('to us') is {{IPA|[d̪ˠuːi̯n̠ʲ]}}.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Sommerfelt|1922|p=150}}</ref>

These all are also a feature of certain Slavic languages such as [[Russian language|Russian]], as well as of [[Lithuanian language|Lithuanian]].{{fact|date=May 2026}}<!--Citation needed for all the details about the phonetic realisations above being present in Russian and Lithuanian, apart from the actual presence of phonemic velarised and palatalised consonants.-->

===Allophones===
{{IPA|/w/}} (written as {{angbr|bh, mh(, v)}}) has two basic [[allophone]]s: the [[labiovelar approximant]] {{IPA|[w]}} and the velarized [[voiced labiodental fricative]] {{IPA|[vˠ]}}. The distribution of these allophones varies from dialect to dialect. In Munster, generally only {{IPA|[vˠ]}} is found,<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Sjoestedt|1931|pp=28–29}}</ref> and in Ulster generally only {{IPA|[w]}} is found.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Quiggin|1906|pp=74–76}}</ref> In Connacht, {{IPA|[w]}} is found word-initially before vowels (e.g. {{lang|ga|bhfuil}} {{IPA|[wɪlʲ]}} 'is') and {{IPA|[vˠ]}} in other positions (e.g. {{lang|ga|naomh}} {{IPA|[n̪ˠiːvˠ]}} 'saint', {{lang|ga|fómhar}} {{IPA|[ˈfˠuːvˠəɾˠ]}} 'autumn', and {{lang|ga|bhrostaigh}} {{IPA|[ˈvˠɾˠɔsˠt̪ˠə]}} 'hurried'<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Finck|1899|pp=64–67}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|de Bhaldraithe|1966|pp=30–31}}</ref>).

The remaining labial fricatives are typically [[labiodental consonant|labiodental]] {{IPA|[fˠ, fʲ, vʲ]}}, but they as well as the fricative allophone {{IPA|[vˠ]}} of {{IPA|/w/}} have [[bilabial consonant|bilabial]] allophones {{IPA|[ɸˠ, ɸʲ, βˠ, βʲ]}} in many dialects; the distribution depends partly on environment (bilabials are more likely to be found adjacent to rounded vowels) and partly on the individual speaker.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|de Bhaldraithe|1966|pp=31–32}}</ref>

Most coronals are [[alveolar consonant|alveolar]], except broad stops and approximants which are typically [[Dental consonant|dental]] {{IPA|[t̪ˠ, d̪ˠ, n̪ˠ, l̪ˠ]}}, and the slender fricative is typically [[postalveolar consonant|postalveolar]] {{IPA|[ʃ]}}. {{IPA|/tʲ, dʲ/}} may be realized as [[alveolo-palatal consonant|alveolo-palatal]] [[affricate]]s {{IPA|[tɕ, dʑ]}} in a number of dialects, including Tourmakeady,<ref>{{Harvcolnb|de Búrca|1958|pp=24–25}}</ref> Erris,<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Mhac an Fhailigh|1968|pp=36–37}}</ref> and Teelin.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Wagner|1959|pp=9–10}}</ref>

{{IPA|/c, ɟ, ɲ/}} may be true [[Palatal consonant|palatals]] {{IPA|[c, ɟ, ɲ]}} or [[velar consonant|palatovelars]] {{IPA|[k̟, ɡ˖, ŋ˖]}}.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Sé|2000|pp=14–15, 18}}</ref>

{{IPA|/j/}} has three allophones in most dialects: a [[palatal approximant]] {{IPA|[j]}} before vowels (except {{IPA|/iː/}}) and [[syllable]]-finally (e.g. {{lang|ga|dheas}} {{IPA|[jasˠ]}} 'nice', {{lang|ga|beidh}} {{IPA|[bʲɛj]}} 'will be'); a [[voiced palatal fricative|voiced (post)palatal fricative]] {{IPA|[ʝ]}} before consonants (e.g. {{lang|ga|ghrian}} {{IPA|[ʝɾʲiən̪ˠ]}} 'sun'); and an intermediate sound {{IPA|[j˔]}} (with more [[fricative consonant|frication]] than {{IPA|[j]}} but less frication than {{IPA|[ʝ]}}) before {{IPA|/iː/}} (e.g. {{lang|ga|dhírigh}} {{IPA|[j˔iːɾʲə]}} 'straightened').<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Breatnach|1947|pp=39–40}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Cuív|1944|pp=42–43}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|de Bhaldraithe|1966|p=34}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Mhac an Fhailigh|1968|pp=34–35}}</ref>

{{IPA|/ɾʲ/}} has the primary allophone {{IPA|[ɹ̝ʲ]}}, a palatalized postalveolar fricative.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Sé|2000|p=19}}</ref>

In many varieties, {{IPA|/x/}} and {{IPA|/ç/}} alternate with {{IPA|/h/}} under a variety of circumstances. For example, as the [[lenition]] of {{IPA|/tʲ/}} and {{IPA|/ʃ/}}, {{IPA|/h/}} is replaced by {{IPA|[ç]}} before back vowels, e.g. {{lang|ga|thabharfainn}} {{IPA|/ˈçuːɾˠhən̠ʲ/}}{{ref|thabharfainn|6}} ('I would give'), {{lang|ga|sheoil}} {{IPA|/çoːlʲ/}} ('drove').<ref>{{Harvcolnb|de Búrca|1958|pp=129–30}}</ref> In Munster, {{IPA|/ç/}} becomes {{IPA|[h]}} after a vowel, e.g. {{lang|ga|fiche}} {{IPA|[ˈfʲɪhə]}} ('twenty').<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Cuív|1944|pp=117–18}}</ref> In Ring, final {{IPA|/h/}} becomes {{IPA|[x]}} in monosyllabic words, e.g. {{lang|ga|scáth}} {{IPA|[sˠkaːx]}} ('fear').<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Breatnach|1947|p=137}}</ref> In some Ulster varieties, e.g. [[Tory Island]], {{IPA|/x/}} can be replaced by {{IPA|[h]}}, e.g. {{lang|ga|cha}} {{IPA|[ha]}} ('not'), be deleted word-finally or before {{IPA|/t̪ˠ/}}, e.g. {{lang|ga|santach}} {{IPA|[ˈsˠan̪ˠt̪ˠa(h)]}} ('greedy') and {{lang|ga|seacht}} {{IPA|[ʃat̪ˠ]}} ('seven').<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Hamilton|1974|p=152}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Searcaigh|1925|p=136}}</ref>

As in [[English language|English]], [[voice (phonetics)|voiceless]] stops are [[aspiration (phonetics)|aspirated]] (articulated with a puff of air immediately upon release) at the start of a word, while voiced stops may be incompletely voiced but are never aspirated. Voiceless [[Stop consonant|stops]] are unaspirated after {{IPA|/sˠ/}} and {{IPA|/ʃ/}} (e.g. {{lang|ga|scanradh}} {{IPA|[sˠkauɾˠə]}}{{ref|scanradh|1}} 'terror'); however, stops remain aspirated after the [[clitic]] ''is'' {{IPA|/sˠ/}} (e.g. {{lang|ga|is cam}} {{IPA|[sˠkʰaum]}} 'it's crooked').<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Breatnach|1947|pp=33, 76}}</ref> Several researchers (e.g. {{Harvnb|Ó Cuív|1944}}, {{Harvnb|Wagner|1959}}, {{Harvnb|de Bhaldraithe|1966}}, {{Harvnb|Mhac an Fhailigh|1968}}, and {{Harvnb|Ó Sé|2000}}) use transcriptions like {{IPA|/sb sd sɡ xd/}}, etc., indicating they consider the stops that occur after voiceless fricatives to be devoiced allophones of the voiced stops rather than unaspirated allophones of the voiceless stops, but this is a minority view.

===Fortis and lenis sonorants===
In [[Old Irish]], the [[sonorant]]s (those spelled {{angbr|l, n, r, m}}) were divided not only into broad and slender types, but also into [[fortis and lenis]] types. The precise phonetic definition of these terms is somewhat vague, but the coronal fortis sounds (spelled {{angbr|l, n, r}}) were probably longer in duration and may have had a larger area of contact between the tongue and the roof of the mouth than the lenis sounds. Fortis {{angbr|m}} was probably a normal {{IPA|[m]}}, while lenis {{angbr|m}} was a nasalized semivowel {{IPA|[w̃]}}, perhaps tending towards a nasalized fricative {{IPA|[ṽ]}} or {{IPA|[β̃]}} when palatalized. By convention, the fortis coronals are transcribed with small capital letters {{angbr IPA|ʟ, ɴ, ʀ}} or [[capital letters]] {{angbr IPA|L, N, R}}, the lenis with lower case {{angbr IPA|l, n, r}} (some authors, such as {{Harvcolnb|Stifter|2006}}, instead use Latin {{angbr IPA|l, n, r, m}} for fortis and Greek {{angbr IPA|λ, ν, ρ, μ}} for lenis). Thus Old Irish had four [[rhotic consonant|rhotic]] phonemes {{IPA|/Rˠ, Rʲ, rˠ, rʲ/}}, four [[lateral consonant|lateral]] phonemes {{IPA|/Lˠ, Lʲ, lˠ, lʲ/}}, and four coronal [[nasal stop|nasal]] phonemes {{IPA|/Nˠ, Nʲ, nˠ, nʲ/}}.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|McCone|1994|p=90}}</ref> Fortis and lenis sonorants contrasted with each other between vowels and word-finally after vowels in Old Irish, e.g. {{lang|sga|berraid}} {{IPA|/bʲeRˠɨðʲ/}} ('he shears') vs. {{lang|sga|beraid}} {{IPA|/bʲerˠɨðʲ/}} ('he may carry'); {{lang|sga|coll}} {{IPA|/koLˠ/}} ('hazel') vs. {{lang|sga|col}} {{IPA|/kolˠ/}} ('sin'); {{lang|sga|sonn}} {{IPA|/sˠoNˠ/}} ('stake') vs. {{lang|sga|son}} {{IPA|/sˠonˠ/}} ('sound').<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Quin|1975|pp=4–5}}</ref> Word-initially, only the fortis sounds were found, but they became lenis in environments where [[Irish initial mutations|morphosyntactically triggered lenition]] was found: {{lang|sga|rún}} {{IPA|/Rˠuːnˠ/}} ('mystery') vs. {{lang|sga|a rún}} {{IPA|/a rˠuːnˠ/}} ('his mystery'), {{lang|sga|lón}} {{IPA|/Lˠoːnˠ/}} ('provision') vs. {{lang|sga|a lón}} {{IPA|/a lˠoːnˠ/}} ('his provision').<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Quin|1975|p=8}}</ref>

In the modern language, the four rhotics have been reduced to two in all dialects, {{IPA|/Rˠ, Rʲ, rˠ/}} having merged as {{IPA|/ɾˠ/}}. For the laterals and nasals, some dialects have kept all four distinct, while others have reduced them to three or two distinct phonemes, as summarized in the following table.

