[[File:Munster Irish vowel chart.svg|class=skin-invert-image|thumb|Vowel phonemes of Munster Irish<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Cuív|1944|p=13}}</ref>]]
[[File:Ulster Irish vowel chart.svg|class=skin-invert-image|thumb|Vowel phonemes of Ulster Irish<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ní Chasaide|1999}}</ref>]]

The [[vowel]] sounds vary from dialect to dialect, but in general Connacht and Munster at least agree in having the [[monophthong]]s {{IPA|/iː/}}, {{IPA|/ɪ/}}, {{IPA|/uː/}}, {{IPA|/ʊ/}}, {{IPA|/eː/}}, {{IPA|/ɛ/}}, {{IPA|/oː/}}, {{IPA|/ɔ/}}, {{IPA|/aː/}}, {{IPA|/a/}}, and [[schwa]] ({{IPA|/ə/}}), which is found only in [[stress (linguistics)|unstressed]] syllables; and the [[diphthong]]s {{IPA|/əi/}}, {{IPA|/əu/}}, {{IPA|/iə/}}, and {{IPA|/uə/}}.

The vowels of [[Ulster Irish]] are more divergent and are not discussed in this article.

===Vowel backness===
The [[vowel backness|backness]] of vowels (that is, the horizontal position of the highest point of the tongue) depends to a great extent on the quality (broad or slender) of adjacent consonants. Some researchers (e.g. {{Harvcolnb|Ó Siadhail|Wigger|1975|pp=80–82}}, {{Harvcolnb|Ó Siadhail|1989|pp=35–37}}, {{Harvnb|Ní Chiosáin|1994}}) have argued that {{IPA|[ɪ]}} and {{IPA|[ʊ]}} are actually allophones of the same phoneme, as are {{IPA|[ɛ]}} and {{IPA|[ɔ]}}, as in a [[vertical vowel system]]. Under this view, these phonemes are [[underspecification|not marked]] at an abstract level as either [[front vowel]]s or [[back vowel]]s. Rather, they acquire a specification for frontness or backness from the consonants around them. In this article, however, the more traditional assumption that {{IPA|/ɪ, ʊ, ɛ, ɔ/}} are four distinct phonemes will be followed. The descriptions of the allophones in this section come from {{Harvcoltxt|Ó Sé|2000|pp=20–24}}; the pronunciations therefore reflect the Munster accent of the [[Dingle Peninsula]]. Unless otherwise noted, however, they largely hold for other Munster and Connacht accents as well.

====Close vowels====
The four [[close vowel]] phonemes of Irish are the fully close {{IPA|/iː/}} and {{IPA|/uː/}}, and the [[near-close vowel|near-close]] {{IPA|/ɪ/}} and {{IPA|/ʊ/}}. Their exact pronunciation depends on the quality of the surrounding consonants. {{IPA|/iː/}} is realized as a [[front vowel|front]] {{IPA|[iː]}} between two slender consonants (e.g. {{lang|ga|tír}} {{IPA|[tʲiːrʲ]}} 'country'). Between a slender and a broad consonant, the tongue is retracted slightly from this position (for which the IPA symbol is {{IPA|[i̠ː]}}), e.g. {{lang|ga|díol}} {{IPA|[dʲi̠ːl̪ˠ]}} ('sale'), {{lang|ga|caoire}} {{IPA|[ki̠ːɾʲə]}} ('berry' gen.). Between two broad consonants, the tongue is retracted even further, almost to the point of being a [[central vowel]] (in IPA, {{IPA|[ïː]}}): {{lang|ga|caora}} {{IPA|[kïːɾˠə]}} ('sheep'). {{IPA|/uː/}} is a fully back {{IPA|[uː]}} between broad consonants (e.g. {{lang|ga|dún}} {{IPA|[d̪ˠuːn̪ˠ]}} 'fort'), but between a broad and a slender consonant, the tongue is somewhat advanced (IPA {{IPA|[u̟ː]}}), e.g. {{lang|ga|triúr}} {{IPA|[tʲɾʲu̟ːɾˠ]}} ('three people'), {{lang|ga|súil}} {{IPA|[sˠu̟ːlʲ]}} ('eye'). Between two slender consonants, it is advanced even further, to a centralized vowel (IPA {{IPA|[üː]}}): {{lang|ga|ciúin}} {{IPA|[cüːnʲ]}} ('quiet').

[[File:Ga close allophones.svg|class=skin-invert-image|thumb|Approximate ranges of the allophones of close vowels]]
The near-close vowels {{IPA|/ɪ/}} and {{IPA|/ʊ/}} show a similar pattern. {{IPA|/ɪ/}} is realized between slender consonants as a front {{IPA|[i̞]}}, e.g. {{lang|ga|tigh}} {{IPA|[tʲi̞ɟ]}} ('house' [[dative case|dat.]]). After a slender consonant and before a broad one, it is a [[near-front vowel|near-front]] {{IPA|[ɪ]}}, e.g. {{lang|ga|giota}} {{IPA|[ˈɟɪt̪ˠə]}} ('piece'). After a broad consonant and before a slender one, it is a more retracted {{IPA|[ɪ̈]}}, e.g. {{lang|ga|tuigeann}} {{IPA|[ˈt̪ˠɪ̈ɟən̪ˠ]}} ('understands'). Finally, between two broad consonants it is a central {{IPA|[ɨ̞]}}, e.g. {{lang|ga|goirt}} {{IPA|[ɡɨ̞ɾˠtʲ]}}{{ref|goirt|2}} ('salty'). {{IPA|/ʊ/}} is a [[near-back vowel|near-back]] {{IPA|[ʊ]}} when all adjacent consonants are broad, e.g. {{lang|ga|dubh}} {{IPA|[d̪ˠʊvˠ]}} ('black'), and a more centralized {{IPA|[ʊ̟]}} after a slender consonant, e.g. {{lang|ga|giobal}} {{IPA|[ˈɟʊ̟bˠəl̪ˠ]}} ('rag').

====Mid vowels====
[[File:Ga mid allophones.svg|class=skin-invert-image|thumb|Approximate ranges of the allophones of mid vowels]]
The realization of the long [[close-mid vowel]]s {{IPA|/eː/}} and {{IPA|/oː/}} varies according to the quality of the surrounding consonants. {{IPA|/eː/}} is a front {{IPA|[eː]}} between two slender consonants (e.g. {{lang|ga|béic}} {{IPA|[bʲeːc]}} 'yell'), a centralized {{IPA|[ëː]}} between a broad and a slender consonant (e.g. {{lang|ga|glaoigh}} {{IPA|[ɡl̪ˠëːɟ]}} 'call'), and a more open centralized {{IPA|[ɛ̝̈ː]}} between two broad consonants (e.g. {{lang|ga|baol}} {{IPA|[bˠɛ̝̈ːl̪ˠ]}} 'danger'). {{IPA|/oː/}} ranges from a back {{IPA|[oː]}} between two broad consonants (e.g. {{lang|ga|fód}} {{IPA|[fˠoːd̪ˠ]}} 'turf') to an advanced {{IPA|[o̟ː]}} between a broad and a slender consonant (e.g. {{lang|ga|fóid}} {{IPA|[fˠo̟ːdʲ]}} 'turf' [gen.]) to a centralized {{IPA|[öː]}} between two slender consonants (e.g. {{lang|ga|ceoil}} {{IPA|[cöːlʲ]}} 'music' [gen.]).

The short [[open-mid vowel]]s also vary depending on their environment. Short {{IPA|/ɛ/}} ranges from a front {{IPA|[ɛ̝]}} between slender consonants (e.g. {{lang|ga|beidh}} {{IPA|[bʲɛ̝ɟ]}} 'will be') to a retracted {{IPA|[ɛ̝̈]}} between a broad and a slender consonant (e.g. {{lang|ga|bead}} {{IPA|[bʲɛ̝̈d̪ˠ]}} 'I will be', {{lang|ga|raibh}} {{IPA|[ɾˠɛ̝̈vʲ]}} 'was') to a central {{IPA|[ɘ̞]}} when the only adjacent consonant is broad (e.g. {{lang|ga|croich}} {{IPA|[kɾˠɘ̞]}} 'cross' [dat.]). Short {{IPA|/ɔ/}} between two broad consonants is usually a back {{IPA|[ɔ̝]}}, e.g. {{lang|ga|cloch}} {{IPA|[kl̪ˠɔ̝x]}} ('stone'), but it is a centralized {{IPA|[ö]}} adjacent to [[nasal stop|nasal consonants]] and [[labial consonant]]s, e.g. {{lang|ga|ansan}} {{IPA|[ən̪ˠˈsˠön̪ˠ]}} ('there') and {{lang|ga|bog}} {{IPA|[bˠöɡ]}} ('soft'). Between a broad and a slender consonant, it is a more open {{IPA|[ɔ̝̈]}}: {{lang|ga|scoil}} {{IPA|[skɔ̝̈lʲ]}} ('school'), {{lang|ga|deoch}} {{IPA|[dʲɔ̝̈x]}} ('drink').

=====Schwa=====

Unstressed {{IPA|/ə/}} is realized as a near-close, near-front {{IPA|[ɪ]}} when adjacent to a [[palatal consonant]], e.g. {{lang|ga|píce}} {{IPA|[ˈpʲiːcɪ]}} ('pike'). Next to other slender consonants, it is a mid-centralized {{IPA|[ɪ̽]}}, e.g. {{lang|ga|sáile}} {{IPA|[ˈsˠaːlʲɪ̽]}} ('salt water'). Adjacent to broad consonants, it is usually a mid central {{IPA|[ə]}}, e.g. {{lang|ga|eolas}} {{IPA|[ˈoːl̪ˠəsˠ]}} ('information'), but when the preceding syllable contains one of the close back vowels {{IPA|/uː, ʊ/}}, it is realized as a mid-centralized back {{IPA|[ʊ̽]}}, e.g. {{lang|ga|dúnadh}} {{IPA|[ˈd̪ˠuːn̪ˠʊ̽]}} ('closing'), {{lang|ga|muca}} {{IPA|[ˈmˠʊkʊ̽]}} ('pigs').

====Open vowels====
[[File:Ga open allophones.svg|class=skin-invert-image|thumb|Approximate ranges of the allophones of open vowels in Munster]]
The realization of the [[open vowel]]s varies according to the quality of the surrounding consonants; there is a significant difference between Munster dialects and Connacht dialects as well. In Munster, long {{IPA|/aː/}} and short {{IPA|/a/}} have approximately the same range of realization: both vowels are relatively back in contact with broad consonants and relatively front in contact with slender consonants. Specifically, long {{IPA|/aː/}} in word-initial position and after broad consonants is a back {{IPA|[ɑː]}}, e.g. {{lang|ga|áit}} {{IPA|[ɑːtʲ]}} ('place'), {{lang|ga|trá}} {{IPA|[t̪ˠɾˠɑː]}} ('beach'). Between a slender and a broad consonant, it is a retracted front {{IPA|[a̠ː]}}, e.g. {{lang|ga|gearrfaidh}} {{IPA|[ˈɟa̠ːɾˠhəɟ]}} ('will cut'), while between two slender consonants it is a fully front {{IPA|[aː]}}, e.g. {{lang|ga|a Sheáin}} {{IPA|[ə çaːnʲ]}} ('John' [[vocative case|voc.]]). In Dingle, the back allophone is rounded to {{IPA|[ɒː]}} after broad labials, e.g. {{lang|ga|bán}} {{IPA|[bˠɒːn̪ˠ]}} ('white'), while in [[Ring, County Waterford]], rounded {{IPA|[ɒː]}} is the usual realization of {{IPA|/aː/}} in all contexts except between slender consonants, where it is a centralized {{IPA|[ɒ̈ː]}}.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Breatnach|1947|pp=12–13}}</ref> Short {{IPA|/a/}} between two slender consonants is a front {{IPA|[a]}}, as in {{lang|ga|gairid}} {{IPA|[ɟaɾʲədʲ]}}{{ref|gairid|3}} ('short'). Between a broad and a slender consonant, it is in most cases a retracted {{IPA|[a̠]}}, e.g. {{lang|ga|fear}} {{IPA|[fʲa̠ɾˠ]}} ('man'), and {{lang|ga|caite}} {{IPA|[ˈka̠tʲə]}} ('worn'), but after broad labials and {{IPA|/l̪ˠ/}} it is a centralized front {{IPA|[ä]}}, e.g. {{lang|ga|baile}} {{IPA|[bˠälʲə]}} ('town'), {{lang|ga|loit}} {{IPA|[l̪ˠätʲ]}}{{ref|loit|4}} ('injure'). When it is adjacent only to broad consonants, it is a centralized back {{IPA|[ɑ̈]}}, e.g. {{lang|ga|mac}} {{IPA|[mˠɑ̈k]}} ('son'), {{lang|ga|abair}} {{IPA|[ɑ̈bˠəɾʲ]}} ('say').

[[File:Ga-CON open allophones.svg|class=skin-invert-image|thumb|Approximate ranges of the allophones of open vowels in Connacht]]
In Connacht varieties,<ref>{{Harvcolnb|de Bhaldraithe|1966|pp=12–14}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|de Búrca|1958|pp=13–14}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Mhac an Fhailigh|1968|pp=13–16}}</ref> the allophones of short {{IPA|/a/}} are consistently further front than the allophones of long {{IPA|/aː/}}. In Erris, for example, short {{IPA|/a/}} ranges from a [[near-open front unrounded vowel|near-open front vowel]] {{IPA|[æ]}} before slender consonants (e.g. {{lang|ga|sail}} {{IPA|[sˠælʲ]}} 'earwax') to an open {{IPA|[a]}} after slender consonants (e.g. {{lang|ga|geal}} {{IPA|[ɟalˠ]}} 'bright') to a centralized back {{IPA|[ɑ̈]}} between broad consonants (e.g. {{lang|ga|capall}} {{IPA|[ˈkɑ̈pˠəl̪ˠ]}} 'horse'). Long {{IPA|/aː/}}, on the other hand, ranges from a back {{IPA|[ɑː]}} between broad consonants (e.g. {{lang|ga|bád}} {{IPA|[bˠɑːd̪ˠ]}} 'boat') to an advanced back {{IPA|[ɑ̟ː]}} before slender consonants (e.g. {{lang|ga|fáil}} {{IPA|[fˠɑ̟ːlʲ]}} 'to get') to a centralized back {{IPA|[ɑ̈ː]}} after slender consonants (e.g. {{lang|ga|breá}} {{IPA|[bʲɾʲɑ̈ː]}} 'fine'). In [[Toormakeady]],<ref>{{Harvcolnb|de Búrca|1958|p=13}}</ref> the back allophone is rounded to {{IPA|[ɒː]}} after broad labials, e.g. {{lang|ga|bán}} {{IPA|[bˠɒːn̪ˠ]}} ('white'). In Connemara, the allophones of {{IPA|/a/}} are lengthened in duration, so that only vowel quality distinguishes the allophones of {{IPA|/a/}} from those of {{IPA|/aː/}}.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|de Bhaldraithe|1966|pp=12–13}}</ref>

====Diphthongs====
[[File:Ga ai-au allophones.svg|class=skin-invert-image|thumb|Approximate ranges of the allophones of {{IPA|/əi/}} and {{IPA|/əu/|cat=no}}]]
The starting point of {{IPA|/əi/}} ranges from a near-open central {{IPA|[ɐ]}} after broad consonants to an open-mid centralized front {{IPA|[ɛ̈]}} after slender consonants, and its end point ranges from a near-close near-front {{IPA|[ɪ]}} before slender consonants to a centralized {{IPA|[ɪ̈]}} before broad consonants.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Breatnach|1947|pp=23–24}}</ref> Examples include {{lang|ga|cladhaire}} {{IPA|[kl̪ˠɐɪɾʲə]}} ('rogue'), {{lang|ga|gadhar}} {{IPA|[ɡɐɪ̈ɾˠ]}} ('dog'), {{lang|ga|cill}} {{IPA|[cɛ̈ɪlʲ]}} ('church'), and {{lang|ga|leigheas}} {{IPA|[lʲɛ̈ɪ̈sˠ]}} ('cure').

The starting point of {{IPA|/əu/}} ranges from a near-open central {{IPA|[ɐ]}} after broad consonants to an open-mid advanced central {{IPA|[ɜ̟]}} after slender consonants, and its end point ranges from a near-close near-back {{IPA|[ʊ]}} before broad consonants to a centralized {{IPA|[ʊ̈]}} before slender consonants.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Breatnach|1947|pp=24–25}}.</ref> Examples include {{lang|ga|bodhar}} {{IPA|[bˠɐʊɾˠ]}} ('deaf'), {{lang|ga|feabhas}} {{IPA|[fʲɜ̟ʊsˠ]}} ('improvement'), {{lang|ga|labhairt}} {{IPA|[l̪ˠɐʊ̈ɾʲtʲ]}} ('speak'), and {{lang|ga|meabhair}} {{IPA|[mʲɜ̟ʊ̈ɾʲ]}} ('memory'). In West Muskerry and the Dingle Peninsula, however, the starting point of {{IPA|/əu/}} is rounded and further back after broad consonants,<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Cuív|1944|p=29}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Sé|2000|p=24}}</ref> e.g. {{lang|ga|gabhar}} {{IPA|[ɡɔʊɾˠ]}} ('goat').

[[File:Ga ia-ua allophones.svg|class=skin-invert-image|thumb|Approximate ranges of the allophones of {{IPA|/iə/}} and {{IPA|/uə/|cat=no}}]]
The starting point of {{IPA|/iə/}} ranges from a close front {{IPA|[i]}} after slender consonants to a retracted {{IPA|[i̠]}} after word-initial broad {{IPA|/ɾˠ/}} (the only context in which it appears after a broad consonant). Its end point ranges from a mid central {{IPA|[ə]}} before broad consonants to a close-mid centralized front {{IPA|[ë]}} before slender consonants.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Sé|2000|p=24}}</ref> Examples include {{lang|ga|ciall}} {{IPA|[ciəl̪ˠ]}} ('sense'), {{lang|ga|riamh}} {{IPA|[ɾˠi̠əvˠ]}} ('ever'), and {{lang|ga|diabhail}} {{IPA|[dʲiëlʲ]}} ('devils').

The starting point of {{IPA|/uə/}} is consistently a close back {{IPA|[u]}} while the end point ranges from {{IPA|[ɐ]}} to {{IPA|[ɪ̽]}}:<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Sé|2000|p=25}}</ref> {{lang|ga|thuas}} {{IPA|[huɐsˠ]}} ('above'), {{lang|ga|uan}} {{IPA|[uən̪ˠ]}} ('lamb'), {{lang|ga|buail}} {{IPA|[bˠuɪ̽lʲ]}} ('strike').

===Nasalized vowels===
In general, vowels in Irish are [[nasalized]] when adjacent to [[nasal consonant]]s. For some speakers, there are reported to be [[minimal pair]]s between [[nasal vowel]]s and oral vowels, indicating that nasal vowels are also separate phonemes; these generally result from an earlier nasalized semivowel {{IPA|[w̃]}} (historically the lenited version of {{IPA|/m/}}), that has since been lost. However, the contrast is not robust in any dialect; most published descriptions say that contrastively nasal vowels are present in the speech of only some (usually older) speakers. Potential minimal pairs include those shown in the table below.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Quiggin|1906|p=65}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Sjoestedt|1931|p=68}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Cuív|1944|p=54}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Sé|2000|p=25}}</ref>

