In addition, where a vowel is nasalized because it is adjacent to a nasal consonant, it often retains its nasalization in related forms where the consonant is no longer nasal. For example, the nasal {{IPA|/m/}} of {{lang|ga|máthair}} {{IPA|[ˈmãːhəɾʲ]}} ('mother') is replaced by nonnasal {{IPA|/w/}} in the phrase {{lang|ga|a mháthair}} {{IPA|[ə ˈwãːhəɾʲ]}} ('his mother'), but the vowel remains nasalized.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Quiggin|1906|p=65}}</ref> Similarly, in {{lang|ga|sneachta}} {{IPA|[ˈʃnʲãxt̪ˠə]}} ('snow') the vowel after the {{IPA|/nʲ/}} is nasalized, while in {{lang|ga|an tsneachta}} {{IPA|[ə ˈtʲɾʲãxt̪ˠə]}} ('the snow' gen.), the {{IPA|/nʲ/}} is replaced by {{IPA|/ɾʲ/}} in some northern dialects, but the nasalized vowel remains.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|de Bhaldraithe|1966|p=46}}</ref>

==Phonotactics==
The most notable aspects of Irish [[phonotactics]] revolve around the behaviour of [[consonant cluster]]s. Here it is important to distinguish between clusters that occur at the beginnings of words and those that occur after vowels, although there is overlap between the two groups.

===Word-initial consonant clusters===
[[File:2C cluster nonmut.svg|thumb|upright=1.36|A summary of the two-consonant clusters available in non-mutation environments]]
[[File:2C cluster mut2.svg|thumb|upright=1.36|A summary of the two-consonant clusters available in mutation environments]]
Irish words can begin with clusters of two or three consonants. In general, all the consonants in a cluster agree in their quality, i.e. either all are broad or all are slender. Two-consonant clusters consist of an [[obstruent consonant]] followed by a [[liquid consonant|liquid]] or [[nasal stop|nasal consonant]] (however, labial obstruents may not be followed by a nasal); examples (from {{Harvcolnb|Ní Chiosáin|1999}}) include {{lang|ga|bleán}} {{IPA|/bʲlʲaːnˠ/}} ('milking'), {{lang|ga|breá}} {{IPA|/bʲɾʲaː/}} ('fine'), {{lang|ga|cnaipe}} {{IPA|/ˈkn̪ˠapʲə/}} ('button'), {{lang|ga|dlí}} {{IPA|/dʲlʲiː/}} ('law'), {{lang|ga|gnáth}} {{IPA|/ɡn̪ˠaː/}} ('usual'), {{lang|ga|pleidhce}} {{IPA|/ˈpʲlʲəicə/}} ('idiot'), {{lang|ga|slios}} {{IPA|/ʃlʲɪsˠ/}} ('slice'), {{lang|ga|sneachta}} {{IPA|/ˈʃnʲaxt̪ˠə/}} ('snow'), {{lang|ga|tlúth}} {{IPA|/t̪ˠl̪ˠuː/}} ('poker'), and {{lang|ga|tnúth}} {{IPA|/t̪ˠn̪ˠuː/}} ('long for'). In addition, {{IPA|/sˠ/}} and {{IPA|/ʃ/}} may be followed by a voiceless stop, as in {{lang|ga|sparán}} {{IPA|/ˈsˠpˠaɾˠaːn̪ˠ/}} ('purse') and {{lang|ga|scéal}} {{IPA|/ʃceːl̪ˠ/}} ('story'). Further, the cluster {{IPA|/mˠn̪ˠ/}} occurs in the word {{lang|ga|mná}} {{IPA|/mˠn̪ˠaː/}} ('women') and a few forms related to it. Three-consonant clusters consist of {{IPA|/sˠ/}} or {{IPA|/ʃ/}} plus a voiceless stop plus a liquid. Examples include {{lang|ga|scliúchas}} {{IPA|/ˈʃclʲuːxəsˠ/}} ('rumpus'), {{lang|ga|scread}} {{IPA|/ʃcɾʲad̪ˠ/}} ('scream'), {{lang|ga|splanc}} {{IPA|/sˠpˠl̪ˠaŋk/}} ('flash'), {{lang|ga|spraoi}} {{IPA|/sˠpˠɾˠiː/}} ('fun'), and {{lang|ga|stríoc}} {{IPA|/ʃtʲɾʲiːk/}} ('streak').

One exception to quality agreement is that broad {{IPA|/sˠ/}} is found before slender labials (and for some speakers in Connemara and Dingle before {{IPA|/c/}} as well<ref>{{Harvcolnb|de Bhaldraithe|1966|p=106}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Sé|2000|p=31}}</ref>). Examples include: {{lang|ga|sméara}} {{IPA|/sˠmʲeːɾˠə/}} ('berries'), {{lang|ga|speal}} {{IPA|/sˠpʲal/}} ('scythe'), {{lang|ga|spleách}} {{IPA|/sˠpʲlʲaːx/}} ('dependent'), {{lang|ga|spreag}} {{IPA|/sˠpʲɾʲaɡ/}} ('inspire'), and {{lang|ga|scéal}} {{IPA|/ʃceːl̪ˠ/ ~ /sˠceːl̪ˠ/}} ('story').

In the environment of an initial [[consonant mutation]], there is a much wider range of possible onset clusters;<ref>{{Harvnb|Ní Chiosáin|1999}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Sé|2000|p=33}}</ref> for example, in a lenition environment the following occur: {{lang|ga|bhlas}} {{IPA|/wl̪ˠasˠ/}} ('tasted'), {{lang|ga|bhris}} {{IPA|/vʲɾʲɪʃ/}} ('broke'), {{lang|ga|chleacht}} {{IPA|/çlʲaxt̪ˠ/}} ('practiced'), {{lang|ga|chrom}} {{IPA|/xɾˠɔmˠ/}} ('bent'), {{lang|ga|ghreamaigh}} {{IPA|/ˈjɾʲamˠə/}} ('stuck'), {{lang|ga|ghníomhaigh}} {{IPA|/ˈjnʲiːwə/}} ('acted'), {{lang|ga|shleamhnaigh}} {{IPA|/hlʲəun̪ˠə/}} ('slipped'), {{lang|ga|shnámh}} {{IPA|/hn̪ˠaːw/}} ('swam'), {{lang|ga|shroich}} {{IPA|/hɾˠɪç/}} ('reached'). In an eclipsis environment, the following are found: {{lang|ga|mbláth}} {{IPA|/mˠl̪ˠaː/}} ('flower'), {{lang|ga|mbliana}} {{IPA|/ˈmʲlʲiən̪ˠə/}} ('years'), {{lang|ga|mbrisfeá}} {{IPA|/ˈmʲɾʲɪʃaː/}} ('you would break'), {{lang|ga|ndlúth}} {{IPA|/n̪ˠl̪ˠuː/}} ('warp'), {{lang|ga|ndroichead}} {{IPA|/ˈn̪ˠɾˠɔhəd̪ˠ/}} ('bridge'), {{lang|ga|ndréimire}} {{IPA|/ˈnʲɾʲeːmʲəɾʲə/}} ('ladder'), {{lang|ga|ngléasfá}} {{IPA|/ˈɲlʲeːsˠaː/}} ('you would dress'), {{lang|ga|ngreadfá}} {{IPA|/ˈɲɾʲat̪ˠaː/}} ('you would leave'), {{lang|ga|ngníomhófá}} {{IPA|/ˈɲnʲiːwoːhaː/}} ('you would act').

In Donegal, Mayo, and Connemara dialects (but not usually on the Aran Islands), the coronal nasals {{IPA|/nˠ, nʲ/}} can follow only {{IPA|/sˠ, ʃ/}} respectively in a word-initial cluster. After other consonants, they are replaced by {{IPA|/ɾˠ, ɾʲ/}}:<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Siadhail|Wigger|1975|pp=116–17}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Siadhail|1989|p=95}}</ref> {{lang|ga|cnoc}} {{IPA|/kɾˠʊk/}} ('hill'), {{lang|ga|mná}} {{IPA|/mˠɾˠaː/}} ('women'), {{lang|ga|gnaoi}} {{IPA|/ɡɾˠiː/}} ('liking'), {{lang|ga|tnúth}} {{IPA|/t̪ˠɾˠuː/}} ('long for').

Under lenition, {{IPA|/sˠn̪ˠ, ʃnʲ/}} become {{IPA|/hn̪ˠ, hnʲ/}} as expected in these dialects, but [[Irish initial mutations#After proclitics|after the definite article ''an'']] they become {{IPA|/t̪ˠɾˠ, tʲɾʲ/}}: {{lang|ga|sneachta}} {{IPA|/ʃnʲaxt̪ˠə/}} ('snow'), {{lang|ga|shneachta}} {{IPA|/hnʲaxt̪ˠə/}} ('snow' [lenited form]), {{lang|ga|an tsneachta}} {{IPA|/ə tʲɾʲaxt̪ˠə/}} ('the snow' gen.).

===Post-vocalic consonant clusters and epenthesis===
[[File:Epenthesis cluster.svg|thumb|Clusters subject to epenthesis]]
Like word-initial consonant clusters, post-vocalic consonant clusters usually agree in broad or slender quality. The only exception here is that broad {{IPA|/ɾˠ/}}, not slender {{IPA|/ɾʲ/}}, appears before the slender coronals {{IPA|/tʲ, dʲ, ʃ, nʲ, lʲ/}}:<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Sé|2000|pp=34–36}}</ref> {{lang|ga|beirt}} {{IPA|/bʲɛɾˠtʲ/}} ('two people'), {{lang|ga|ceird}} {{IPA|/ceːɾˠdʲ/}} ('trade'), {{lang|ga|doirse}} {{IPA|/ˈd̪ˠoːɾˠʃə/}} ('doors'), {{lang|ga|doirnín}} {{IPA|/d̪ˠuːɾˠˈnʲiːnʲ/}} ('handle'), {{lang|ga|comhairle}} {{IPA|/ˈkuːɾˠlʲə/}} ('advice').

A cluster of {{IPA|/ɾˠ, ɾʲ/}}, {{IPA|/l̪ˠ, lʲ/}}, or {{IPA|/n̪ˠ, nʲ/}} followed by a labial or dorsal consonant (except the voiceless stops {{IPA|/pˠ, pʲ/}}, {{IPA|/k, c/}}) is broken up by an [[epenthesis|epenthetic]] vowel {{IPA|/ə/}}:<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ní Chiosáin|1999}}</ref> {{lang|ga|borb}} {{IPA|/ˈbˠɔɾˠəbˠ/}} ('abrupt'), {{lang|ga|gorm}} {{IPA|/ˈɡɔɾˠəmˠ/}} ('blue'), {{lang|ga|dearmad}} {{IPA|/ˈdʲaɾˠəmˠəd̪ˠ/}} ('mistake'), {{lang|ga|dearfa}} {{IPA|/ˈdʲaɾˠəfˠə/}} ('certain'), {{lang|ga|seirbhís}} {{IPA|/ˈʃɛɾʲəvʲiːʃ/}} ('service'), {{lang|ga|fearg}} {{IPA|/ˈfʲaɾˠəɡ/}} ('anger'), {{lang|ga|dorcha}} {{IPA|/ˈd̪ˠɔɾˠəxə/}} ('dark'), {{lang|ga|dalba}} {{IPA|/ˈd̪ˠal̪ˠəbˠə/}} ('bold'), {{lang|ga|colm}} {{IPA|/ˈkɔl̪ˠəmˠ/}} ('dove'), {{lang|ga|soilbhir}} {{IPA|/ˈsˠɪlʲəvʲəɾʲ/}} ('pleasant'), {{lang|ga|gealbhan}} {{IPA|/ˈɟal̪ˠəwən̪ˠ/}} ('sparrow'), {{lang|ga|binb}} {{IPA|/ˈbʲɪnʲəbʲ/}} ('venom'), {{lang|ga|Banbha}}, {{IPA|/ˈbˠan̪ˠəwə/}} (a name for Ireland), {{lang|ga|ainm}} {{IPA|/ˈanʲəmʲ/}} ('name'), {{lang|ga|meanma}} {{IPA|/ˈmʲan̪ˠəmˠə/}} ('mind'), {{lang|ga|ainmhí}} {{IPA|/ˈanʲəvʲiː/}} ('animal').

There is no epenthesis, however, if the vowel preceding the cluster is long or a diphthong: {{lang|ga|fáirbre}} {{IPA|/ˈfˠaːɾʲbʲɾʲə/}} ('wrinkle'), {{lang|ga|téarma}} {{IPA|/ˈtʲeːɾˠmˠə/}} ('term'), {{lang|ga|léargas}} {{IPA|/ˈlʲeːɾˠɡəsˠ/}} ('insight'), {{lang|ga|dualgas}} {{IPA|/ˈd̪ˠuəl̪ˠɡəsˠ/}} ('duty'). There is also no epenthesis into words that are at least three syllables long: {{lang|ga|firmimint}} {{IPA|/ˈfʲɪɾʲmʲəmʲənʲtʲ/}} ('firmament'), {{lang|ga|smiolgadán}} {{IPA|/ˈsˠmʲɔl̪ˠɡəd̪ˠaːn̪ˠ/}} ('throat'), {{lang|ga|caisearbhán}} {{IPA|/ˈkaʃəɾˠwaːn̪ˠ/}} ('dandelion'), {{lang|ga|Cairmilíteach}} {{IPA|/ˈkaɾʲmʲəlʲiːtʲəx/}} ('Carmelite').

==Phonological processes==

===Vowel-initial words===
Vowel-initial words in Irish exhibit behaviour that has led linguists to suggest that the vowel sound they begin with on the surface is not actually the first sound in the word at a more abstract level. Specifically, when a clitic ending in a consonant precedes a word beginning with the vowel, the consonant of the clitic surfaces as either broad or slender, depending on the specific word in question. For example, the {{Anglebracket|n}} of the [[definite article]] {{lang|ga|an}} ('the') is slender before the word {{lang|ga|iontais}} ('wonder') but broad before the word {{lang|ga|aois}} ('age'):<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ní Chiosáin|1991|pp=80–82}}</ref>{{lang|ga|an iontais}} {{IPA|/ənʲ&nbsp;ˈiːn̪ˠt̪ˠəʃ/}} ('the wonder' gen.) vs. {{lang|ga|an aois}} {{IPA|/ən̪ˠ&nbsp;ˈiːʃ/}} ('the age').

One analysis of these facts<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ní Chiosáin|1991|p=83}}</ref> is that vowel-initial words actually begin, at an abstract level of representation, with a kind of "empty" consonant that consists of nothing except the information "broad" or "slender". Another analysis is that vowel-initial words, again at an abstract level, all begin with one of two [[semivowel]]s, one triggering palatalization and the other triggering velarization of a preceding consonant.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Siadhail|Wigger|1975|pp=98–99}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Siadhail|1989|pp=64–65}}</ref>

===Lengthening before fortis sonorants===
Where reflexes of the Old Irish [[#Fortis and lenis sonorants|fortis sonorants]] appear in syllable-final position (in some cases, only in word-final position), they trigger a lengthening or diphthongization of the preceding vowel in most dialects of Irish.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|O'Rahilly|1932|pp=49–52}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Siadhail|Wigger|1975|pp=89–94}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Siadhail|1989|pp=49–50}}</ref><ref>{{Harvnb|Carnie|2002}}</ref> The details vary from dialect to dialect.

In Donegal and Mayo, lengthening is found only before {{Anglebracket|rd, rl, rn}}, before {{Anglebracket|rr}} (except when a vowel follows), and in a few words also before word-final {{Anglebracket|ll}},<ref>{{Harvcolnb|de Búrca|1958|pp=132–34}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Mhac an Fhailigh|1968|pp=163–64}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Evans|1969|p=127}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Baoill|1996|p=16}}</ref> e.g. {{lang|ga|barr}} {{IPA|/bˠaːɾˠ/}} ('top'), {{lang|ga|ard}} {{IPA|/aːɾˠd̪ˠ/}} ('tall'), {{lang|ga|orlach}} {{IPA|/ˈoːɾˠl̪ˠax/}} ('inch'), {{lang|ga|tuirne}} {{IPA|/ˈt̪uːɾˠn̠ʲə/}} ('spinning wheel'), {{lang|ga|thall}} {{IPA|/haːl̪ˠ/}} ('yonder').

In Connemara,<ref>{{Harvcolnb|de Bhaldraithe|1966|pp=109–12}}</ref> the Aran Islands,<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Finck|1899}}</ref> and Munster,<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Breatnach|1947|pp=142–44}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Cuív|1944|pp=121–23}}</ref> lengthening is found generally not only in the environments listed above, but also before {{Anglebracket|nn}} (unless a vowel follows) and before word-final {{Anglebracket|m, ng}}. For example, the word {{lang|ga|poll}} ('hole') is pronounced {{IPA|/pˠəul̪ˠ/}} in all of these regions, while {{lang|ga|greim}} ('grip') is pronounced {{IPA|/ɟɾʲiːmʲ/}} in Connemara and Aran and {{IPA|/ɟɾʲəimʲ/}} in Munster.

Because vowels behave differently before broad sonorants than before slender ones in many cases, and because there is generally no lengthening (except by [[Analogy#Linguistics|analogy]]) when the sonorants are followed by a vowel, there is a variety of vowel [[alternation (linguistics)|alternations]] between different related word-forms. For example, in Dingle<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Sé|2000|pp=40–42}}</ref> {{lang|ga|ceann}} ('head') is pronounced {{IPA|/cəun̪ˠ/}} with a diphthong, but {{lang|ga|cinn}} (the [[genitive]] singular of the same word) is pronounced {{IPA|/ciːnʲ/}} with a long vowel, while {{lang|ga|ceanna}} (the plural, meaning 'heads') is pronounced {{IPA|/ˈcan̪ˠə/}} with a short vowel.

This lengthening has received a number of different explanations within the context of theoretical phonology. All accounts agree that some property of the fortis sonorant is being transferred to the preceding vowel, but the details about what property that is vary from researcher to researcher. {{Harvcoltxt|Ó Siadhail|Wigger|1975|pp=89–90}}<ref>also repeated in {{Harvcolnb|Ó Siadhail|1989|pp=48–50}}</ref> argue that the fortis sonorant is [[tenseness|tense]] (a term only vaguely defined phonetically) and that this tenseness is transferred to the vowel, where it is realized phonetically as vowel length and/or diphthongization. {{Harvcoltxt|Ní Chiosáin|1991|pp=188–95}} argues that the triggering consonant is underlyingly associated with a unit of [[syllable weight]] called a [[mora (linguistics)|''mora'']]; this mora then shifts to the vowel, creating a long vowel or a diphthong. {{Harvcoltxt|Carnie|2002}} expands on that analysis to argue that the fortis sonorants have an [[advanced tongue root]] (that is, the bottom of the tongue is pushed upward during articulation of the consonant) and that diphthongization is an [[articulatory phonetics|articulatory]] effect of this tongue movement.

===Devoicing===
Where a voiced [[obstruent]] or {{IPA|/w/}} comes into contact with {{IPA|/h/}}, the {{IPA|/h/}} is absorbed into the other sound, which then becomes voiceless (in the case of {{IPA|/w/}}, devoicing is to {{IPA|/fˠ/}}). Devoicing is found most prominently in the [[future tense|future]] of [[Irish verbs#First conjugation|first conjugation]] verbs (where {{IPA|/h/}} is spelled {{Anglebracket|f}}) and in the formation of [[verbal adjective]]s (where {{IPA|/h/}} is spelled {{Anglebracket|th}}). For example, the verb {{lang|ga|scuab}} {{IPA|/sˠkuəbˠ/}} ('sweep') ends in the voiced consonant {{IPA|/bˠ/}}, but its future tense {{lang|ga|scuabfaidh}} {{IPA|/ˈsˠkuəpˠəɟ/}} ('will sweep') and verbal adjective {{lang|ga|scuabtha}} {{IPA|/ˈsˠkuəpˠə/}} ('swept') have the voiceless consonant {{IPA|/pˠ/}}.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Breatnach|1947|pp=137–38}}</ref>

===Sandhi===
Irish exhibits a number of external [[sandhi]] effects, i.e. phonological changes across word boundaries, particularly in rapid speech. The most common type of sandhi in Irish is [[Assimilation (linguistics)|assimilation]], which means that a sound changes its pronunciation in order to become more similar to an adjacent sound. One type of assimilation in Irish is found when a coronal consonant ({{Anglebracket|d, l, n, r, s, t}}) changes from being broad to being slender before a word that begins with a slender coronal consonant and vice versa. For example, {{lang|ga|feall}} {{IPA|/fʲal̪ˠ/}} ('deceive') ends with a broad {{Anglebracket|ll}}, but in the phrase {{lang|ga|d'fheall sé orm}} {{IPA|[dʲal̠ʲ ʃə ɔɾˠəmˠ]}} ('it deceived me'), the {{Anglebracket|ll}} has become slender because the following word, {{lang|ga|sé}}, starts with a slender coronal consonant.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Quiggin|1906|pp=146–50}}</ref>

{{Anglebracket|n(n)}} may also assimilate to the [[place of articulation]] of a following consonant, becoming labial before a labial consonant, palatal before a palatal consonant, and velar before a velar consonant.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|de Búrca|1958|pp=65–68}}</ref> For example, {{Anglebracket|nn}} of {{lang|ga|ceann}} {{IPA|/can̪ˠ/}} ('one') becomes {{IPA|[mˠ]}} in {{lang|ga|ceann bacach}} {{IPA|[camˠ ˈbˠakəx]}} ('a lame one') and {{IPA|[ŋ]}} in {{lang|ga|ceann carrach}} {{IPA|[caŋ ˈkaɾˠəx]}} ('a scabbed one'). A voiced consonant at the end of a word may devoice when the next word begins with a voiceless consonant,<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Finck|1899|pp=123–24}}</ref> as in {{lang|ga|lúb sé}} {{IPA|[l̪ˠuːpˠ ʃeː]}} ('he bent'), where {{IPA|/bˠ/}} of {{lang|ga|lúb}} {{IPA|/l̪ˠuːbˠ/}} ('bent') became {{IPA|[pˠ]}} before the voiceless {{IPA|/ʃ/}} of {{lang|ga|sé}}.

==Stress==

In Irish, words normally have only one [[stress (linguistics)|stressed]] syllable (ˈ◌). Outside of Munster this is usually the first syllable of the word, e.g. {{lang|ga|d'imigh}} {{IPA|/ˈdʲɪmʲiː/}} ('left' [verb]) and {{lang|ga|easonóir}} {{IPA|/ˈasˠən̪ˠoːɾʲ/}} ('dishonor').<ref>
{{Harvcolnb|de Búrca|1958|pp=74–75}}</ref> However, certain words, especially [[adverb]]s and [[loanword]]s, have stress on a noninitial syllable, e.g. {{lang|ga|amháin}} {{IPA|/əˈwaːnʲ/}} ('only'), {{lang|ga|tobac}} {{IPA|/təˈbak/}} ('tobacco').

===Compound words===
In most [[compound (linguistics)|compound]] words, there are four possible stress patterns:<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Liatháin|Nic Mhaoláin|Ní Bhrádaigh|Ní Ící|1998|pp=416–17}}</ref>
<ol>
  <li>In most compounds, primary stress falls on the first member and a [[secondary stress]] (ˌ◌) falls on the second member, e.g. {{lang|ga|lagphortach}} {{IPA|/ˈl̪ˠaɡˌfˠɔɾˠt̪ˠəx/}} ('spent bog').</li>
  <li>In compounds beginning with {{lang|ga|do-}}, {{lang|ga|so-}} and {{lang|ga|in-}} ('possible'), the prefix takes secondary stress, while the following element takes primary stress, e.g. {{lang|ga|dothuigthe}} {{IPA|/ˌdˠoˈhɪkʲə/}} ('unintelligible').</li>
  <li>In compounds beginning with {{lang|ga|an-}} (intensive prefix), {{lang|ga|bith-}} ('perpetual'), {{lang|ga|colg-, comh-, dian-, glan-, gnáth-, lán-}} and {{lang|ga|príomh-}}, both the prefix and the following syllable take primary stress, e.g. {{lang|ga|an-mhaith}} {{IPA|/ˈanˈwa/}} ('very good'). Note that {{lang|ga|an-}} (meaning 'in-, un-') and {{lang|ga|bith-}} (meaning 'bio-') follow the group 1 (primary-secondary) stress pattern.</li>
  <li>The last group ({{lang|ga|ard-, dearg-, droch-, fíor-, iar-, ró-, síor-}}) show a mix of:
    <ol type="i">
      <li>variation between primary-primary and primary-secondary stress, e.g. {{lang|ga|Fíor-Dhia}} {{IPA|/ˈfʲiːɾˠ⁠ˈjiːə/}} ('true God') vs. {{lang|ga|fíoruisce}} {{IPA|/ˈfʲiːɾˠˌɪʃcə/}} ('spring water')</li>
      <li>variation between primary-secondary and primary-zero stress, particularly in old compounds now treated as simple words, e.g. {{lang|ga|goltraí}} {{IPA|/ˈgɔl̪ˠt̪ˠɾˠiː/}} ('sorrowful music, lament'), {{lang|ga|suantraí}} {{IPA|/ˈsuːən̪ˠt̪ˠɾˠiː/}} ('lullaby').</li>
      <li>variation in stress pattern depending on whether the second element is a noun or adjective, e.g. primary-primary {{lang|ga|droch-dhuine}} {{IPA|/ˈd̪ˠɾˠɔx⁠ˈɣinʲə/}} ('bad person') vs. primary-secondary {{lang|ga|drochbhéasach}} {{IPA|/ˈd̪ˠɾˠɔx⁠ˌvʲeːsəx/}} ('bad-mannered'), primary-primary {{lang|ga|Ard-Easpag}} {{IPA|/ˈaːɾˠd̪ˠ⁠ˈæsˠpˠəɡ/}} ('Archbishop') vs. primary-secondary {{lang|ga|ardnósach}} {{IPA|/ˈaːɾˠd̪ˠˌn̪ˠõːsəx/}} ('bad-mannered')</li>
    </ol>
</ol>

===Munster===
In Munster, stress is attracted to a long vowel or diphthong in the second or third syllable of a word, e.g. {{lang|ga|cailín}} {{IPA|/kaˈlʲiːnʲ/}} ('girl'), {{lang|ga|achainí}} {{IPA|/axəˈnʲiː/}} ('request').<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Sé|2000|pp=46–47}}</ref> In the now-extinct accent of East Mayo, stress was attracted to a long vowel or diphthong in the same way as in Munster; in addition, stress was attracted to a short vowel before word-final {{Anglebracket|ll, m, nn}} when that word was also final in its utterance.<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Lavin|1957}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Dillon|1973}}</ref><ref>{{Harvcolnb|Green|1997|pp=86–90}}</ref> For example, {{lang|ga|capall}} ('horse') was pronounced {{IPA|[kaˈpˠɞl̪ˠ]}} in isolation or as the last word of a sentence, but as {{IPA|[ˈkapˠəl̪ˠ]}} in the middle of a sentence.

In Munster, stress is attracted to {{IPA|/a/}} in the second syllable of a word if it is followed by {{IPA|/x/}}, ''provided'' the first syllable (and third syllable, if there is one) contains a short vowel. Examples include {{lang|ga|bacach}} {{IPA|/bˠəˈkax/}} ('lame') and {{lang|ga|slisneacha}} {{IPA|/ʃlʲəˈʃnʲaxə/}} ('chips'). However, if the first or third syllable contains a long vowel or diphthong, stress is attracted to that syllable instead, and the {{IPA|/a/}} before {{IPA|/x/}} is reduced to {{IPA|/ə/}} as normal, e.g. {{lang|ga|éisteacht}} {{IPA|/ˈeːʃtʲəxt̪ˠ/}} ('listen'), {{lang|ga|moltachán}} {{IPA|/ˌmˠɔl̪ˠhəˈxaːn̪ˠ/}}{{ref|moltachán|5}} ('wether').<ref>{{Harvcolnb|Ó Cuív|1944|p=66}}</ref>

